hadiths[82]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1798","[AI-translation] From Hakim ibn Hizam, may Allah be pleased with him, that he asked the Prophet, peace be upon him: ""Which charity is best?"" He said: ""Begin with those you support, and charity given out of self-sufficiency."
"1799","[AI-translation] or from Tha'laba - from his father, that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""Give a sa' of wheat or grain shared between two, or a sa' of dates, or a sa' of barley for every free person, slave, minor, and adult."
"1800","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""Give a sa' of food."
"1801","[AI-translation] From Zayd ibn Aslam (from his father) he said: There was a man among the people of Sham who was pleasing, so 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""For what reason do the people of Sham love you?"" He said: ""I fight alongside them and share with them."" He said: So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, offered him ten thousand and said: ""Take it and use it to help you in your fighting."" He said: ""I am free of need from it."" 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, offered me wealth less than what I offered you, and I said to him what you said to me. He said: 'If Allah gives you wealth that you did not ask for, nor did your soul desire it, then accept it, for it is the provision of Allah that Allah has driven to you.'"
"1802","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: ""If Allah gives one of you good, let him begin with himself and the people of his household."
"1803","[AI-translation] Badri, raised to the Prophet: ""When a man spends on his family an expenditure seeking reward, it is a charity for him."
"1804","[AI-translation] From Qubaysah ibn Dhu'ayb: That 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, gave (Ibn) al-Sa'di a thousand dinars, but he refused to accept them and said: ""I am free of need from it."" 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""I will say to you what the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to me: 'If Allah drives provision to you without asking and without the soul aspiring to it, then take it, for Allah has given it to you.'"
"1805","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, raised to the Prophet: ""If a man owns a woman, her gift is not valid except with his permission."
"1806","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, raised to him: ""Seek independence from people, even if by brushing with a siwak."
"1807","[AI-translation] From Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: ""The best charity is the effort of the one with little, and begin with those you support."
"1808","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: The best charity is the gifted animal, which yields a bowl of milk in the morning and a bowl of milk in the evening."
"1809","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Hurairah, reaching back to the Prophet: Is there not a man who would gift a she-camel (from his camels) to a household (that has no milk), which yields a bowl of milk in the morning and a bowl of milk in the evening? Indeed, its reward is great."
"1810","[AI-translation] Muharibi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, advise me."" He said, ""Restrain your hand."" And in a narration, ""Do not stretch forth your hand except toward good."
"1811","On the authority of Amr ibn Taghlib: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was brought some wealth or captives, so he distributed it. He gave to some men and left out others. It reached him that those who were left out were displeased. So he praised Allah, then extolled Him, then said: ""As for what follows: By Allah, I give to one man and leave another, and the one I leave is more beloved to me than the one I give. But I give to some people because of the anxiety and fear I see in their hearts, and I entrust some people to the richness and goodness that Allah has placed in their hearts."" Among them was Amr ibn Taghlib. Amr said: ""By Allah, I would not love to have the choicest camels in exchange for that statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."""
"1812","Adi ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) when two men came to him. One of them complained of poverty, and the other complained of highway robbery. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""As for highway robbery, it will not be long before a caravan goes out to Mecca without any guard. As for poverty, the Hour will not be established until one of you goes around with his charity, not finding anyone to accept it from him. Then one of you will surely stand before Allah, with no screen between Him and him, nor any interpreter to translate for him. Then He will say to him: 'Did I not give you wealth?' He will say: 'Yes.' Then He will say: 'Did I not send a messenger to you?' He will say: 'Yes.' Then he will look to his right and see nothing but the Fire, then look to his left and see nothing but the Fire. So let each of you protect yourselves from the Fire, even if only with half a date. If he cannot find that, then with a good word."""
"1813","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Prophet, peace be upon him, say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: O son of Adam, if you give the surplus it is better for you, and if you withhold it it is worse for you. Begin with those you support, and Allah does not blame for sufficiency. And the upper hand is better for you than the lower hand."
"1814","[AI-translation] Narrated from Ibn Udhnanah who said: I advanced Alqamah two thousand dirhams. When his stipend was issued, I said to him, ""Pay me."" He said, ""Delay me until next year."" So I came to him and took it. He said: I came to him afterwards. He said, ""You have distressed me; you withheld it from me."" I said, ""Yes, it is your deed."" He said, ""And what is my concern?"" I said, ""You narrated to me from Ibn Mas'ud that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: An advance loan runs the course of half a charity."" He said, ""Yes, it is thus."" He said: ""So take it now."
"1815","[AI-translation] Narrated from Khabbab who said: He was cauterized with seven cauterizations, so we went to visit him. He said, ""Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say, 'Do not wish for death,' I would have wished for it."" And while he was repairing a wall of his, he said, ""I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, say: 'A man is rewarded for all his expenditure except in this dust.'"
"1816","[AI-translation] Narrated from Uqbah bin Amir, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: Charity extinguishes the heat of the grave for its owner, and the believer will seek shade on the Day of Resurrection in the shade of his charity."
"1817","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace be upon him, sent a man from Banu Makhzum to collect charity. He said to Abu Rafi, ""Accompany me so that you may obtain some of it."" He said, ""No, until I go to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and ask him."" So he went to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and asked him, and he said, ""Charity is not lawful for us, and the mawlas of a people are from themselves."
"1818","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: The likeness of the one who takes back his gift is like that of a dog which ate until it was full, then vomited, and then returned to its vomit and ate it."
"1819","[AI-translation] He said, peace be upon him: ""Divine assistance comes from Allah in accordance with the burden, and patience comes from Allah in accordance with the trial."
"1820","[AI-translation] Narrated from Thawban, elevated to the Prophet: ""Indeed, among my Ummah are those who, if one of you came to him asking for a dinar, he would not give it to him; if he asked for a dirham, he would not give it to him; if he asked for a fals, he would not give it to him. Yet if he were to ask Allah for Paradise, He would give it to him. He is one wearing two shabby garments, unnoticed; if he were to swear an oath upon Allah, He would surely fulfill it for him."
"1821","[AI-translation] Hadrami bin Kulthum bin 'Alqama bin Najiya bin al-Harith al-Khuza'i, from his grandfather Kulthum, from his father, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said to them in the Year of al-Muraysi' when they embraced Islam: ""Part of the completion of your Islam is that you pay the zakat on your wealth."
"1822","[AI-translation] Narrated from Hakim bin Hizam who said: Muhammad (peace be upon him) was the most beloved of men to me during the pre-Islamic period. When he was sent as a prophet and migrated to al-Madinah, Hakim bin Hizam attended the market season while he was still a disbeliever. He found a garment from Dhu Yazan being sold, so he bought it for fifty dinars to gift it to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He brought it to him in al-Madinah and wanted him to take it as a gift, but he refused. 'Ubaid Allah said: I think he said: ""We do not accept anything from the polytheists, but if you wish, we will take it for its price."" So I gave it to him when he refused the gift."
"1823","[AI-translation] He (peace be upon him) said: ""Spend, O Bilal! And do not fear from the Owner of the Throne any decrease."
"1824","[AI-translation] Narrated from 'Aisha, elevated to the Prophet: Every human being from the children of Adam is created upon three hundred and sixty joints. So whoever declares the greatness of Allah, praises Allah, declares His oneness, declares His perfection, and seeks forgiveness from Allah, and removes a stone from the path of the people, or a thorn, or a bone from the path of the people, and enjoins good or forbids evil, to the number of those three hundred and sixty joints, then he walks that day having removed himself from the Fire."
"1825","[AI-translation] Narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I give to Quraysh, seeking to win their hearts, for they are recent in their departure from pre-Islamic ignorance."
"1826","[AI-translation] Narrated from 'Amr bin Taghlib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) gave to some people and withheld from others. It was as if they found fault with him for this, so he said: ""I give to a people out of fear of their wavering and their anxiety, and I entrust other people to what Allah has placed in their hearts of (self-sufficiency and) goodness, among them being 'Amr bin Taghlib."" 'Amr bin Taghlib said: ""I would not love to have red camels in exchange for the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."
"1827","[AI-translation] Rahman bin 'Abdullah bin Ka'b bin Malik al-Sulami: That 'Amir bin Malik bin Ja'far, who was called Mul'ib al-Asinna, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) while he was a polytheist. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) presented Islam to him, but he refused to embrace Islam, and he presented a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I do not accept the gift of a polytheist."
"1828","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Umama and others among the Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him), from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: Any Muslim man who frees a Muslim man, that will be his ransom from the Fire; every part of him will expiate for every part of him. 2- And any Muslim man who frees two Muslim women, they will be his ransom from the Fire; every part of them will expiate for every part of him. 3- And any Muslim woman who frees a Muslim woman, she will be her ransom from the Fire; every part of her will expiate for every part of her."
"1829","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I asked the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Which deed is best?"" He said: ""Faith in Allah and striving in His path."" I said: ""Which slaves are best to free?"" He said: ""The most expensive of them, and in a narration: the one with the highest price and most precious to his family."" I said: ""What if I cannot do that?"" He said: ""You help a craftsman or work for one who is unskilled."" He said: ""What if I cannot do that?"" He said: ""You refrain from harming people, for it is a charity you give to yourself."
"1830","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Abu Talha was the wealthiest of the Ansar in Medina in terms of palm trees, and the most beloved of his wealth to him was Bayruha. It was situated opposite the mosque, and Allah's Messenger (peace be upon him) would enter it and drink from the pleasant water therein.Anas said: When this verse was revealed: ""Never will you attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love"" (Ali 'Imran: 92), Abu Talha stood up before Allah's Messenger (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Allah's Messenger, indeed Allah the Blessed and Exalted says: 'Never will you attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love,' and indeed the most beloved of my wealth to me is Bayruha, and it is a charity for Allah; I hope for its righteousness and reward with Allah. So place it, O Allah's Messenger, wherever Allah shows you.""He said: Allah's Messenger (peace be upon him) said: ""Excellent! That is a profitable wealth, that is a profitable wealth. And I have heard what you said, and I think you should put it among your relatives."""
"1831","[AI-translation] From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The charities of the Muslims are taken at their watering places."" Meaning: their livestock."
"1832","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was brought the funeral of a man from the Ansar, so he prayed over him, then said: ""What did he leave?"" They said: ""He left two or three dinars."" He said: ""He left two or three kiyat."
"1833","[AI-translation] From Sa'id ibn Jubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not give charity except to the people of your religion."" Then Allah, Exalted be He, revealed: *Not upon you is their guidance...* up to His saying: *And whatever you spend of good, it will be repaid to you.* The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Give charity to the people of the religions."
"1834","[AI-translation] The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Give charity and do not hoard, lest it be hoarded from you."" This comes from the hadith of Asma' and 'A'ishah. And the wording of the hadith of Asma' (and she used to count it) she said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), I have no wealth except what al-Zubayr brings to me, so should I give charity?' He said: 'Give charity and do not hoard, lest it be hoarded from you.'"
"1835","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The dinar is a treasure, the dirham is a treasure, and the qirat is a treasure."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, as for the dinar and the dirham we know them, but what is the qirat?"" He said: ""Half a dirham, half a dirham, half a dirham."
"1836","[AI-translation] From 'Uqbah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace be upon him) in al-Madinah the 'Asr prayer. He gave the salam, then stood up quickly, stepping over the necks of the people to some of his wives' chambers. The people were startled by his speed. He came out to them and saw that they were amazed by his speed, so he said: ""I remembered (while I was in the prayer) some gold dust (from charity) that was with us, and I disliked that it should detain me (in another narration: that it should remain overnight - or stay - with us), so I ordered it to be distributed."
"1837","[AI-translation] Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) only prescribed zakat on these four: wheat, barley, raisins, and dates."
"1838","On the authority of al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḍamrī, that Umm al-Ḥakam or Ḍubāʿah, the two daughters of al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, narrated to him, from one of them, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) received some captives. So I went, along with my sister and Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and his family and grant them peace), and we complained to him of our situation and asked him to order something for us from the captives. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The orphans of Badr have preceded you, but I will guide you to something that is better for you than that: glorify Allah (say Allāhu Akbar) after every prayer thirty-three times, praise Allah (say Subḥān Allāh) thirty-three times, and thank Allah (say Al-ḥamdu lillāh) thirty-three times, and say: 'There is no deity but Allah alone, He has no partner; to Him belongs the dominion and to Him belongs all praise, and He is over all things Omnipotent.'"""
"1839","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The worst thing in a man is devouring greed and extreme cowardice."
"1840","[AI-translation] He (peace be upon him) said: ""Secret charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord."
"1841","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: ""Upon every joint of the joints of the children of Adam there is a charity."
"1842","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, wrote to the people of Yemen, to Al-Harith bin 'Abd Kalal and whoever was with him of the people of Yemen from Ma'afir and Hamdan: ""Upon the believers, from the charity of fruits - or the wealth of real estate - is one-tenth of what the spring waters and the sky waters, and upon what is watered by the bucket is half of one-tenth."
"1843","[AI-translation] Muzani, marfu'an: In camels there is a far', and in sheep there is a far'."
"1844","[AI-translation] Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, marfu'an: He used to go out on the Day of Adha and the Day of Fitr, and he would begin with the prayer. When he had prayed his prayer and given salam, he would stand (standing) (on his feet) and face the people (with his face) while they were seated in their prayer place. If he had a need concerning an expedition, he would mention it to the people, or if he had a need other than that, he would enjoin it upon them. And he used to say: ""Give in charity, give in charity, give in charity."" And the ones who gave charity the most were women, then he would depart."
"1845","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, marfu'an: Every joint of a person must give charity each day the sun rises: to judge justly between two is charity; to help a man with his mount, lifting him onto it or hoisting his belongings onto it, is charity; a good word is charity; every step one takes to the prayer is charity; and removing harm from the road is charity."
"1846","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, marfu'an: There is no zakat on horses and slaves, except Zakat Al-Fitr for the slaves."
"1847","On the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""There is no zakat due on less than five camels, and nothing on four. When they reach five, a sheep is due until they reach nine. When they reach ten, two sheep are due until they reach fourteen. When they reach fifteen, three sheep are due until they reach nineteen. When they reach twenty, four sheep are due until they reach twenty-four. When they reach twenty-five, a bint makhad (one-year-old female camel) is due up to thirty-five. If a bint makhad is not available, then an ibn labun (two-year-old male camel). If the number increases by one camel, a bint labun (two-year-old female camel) is due until they reach forty-five. If the number increases by one camel, a hiqqah (three-year-old female camel) is due until they reach sixty. If the number increases by one camel, a jadh'ah (four-year-old female camel) is due until they reach seventy-five. If the number increases by one camel, two bint labun are due until they reach ninety. If the number increases by one camel, two hiqqah are due until they reach one hundred and twenty. Thereafter, for every fifty camels a hiqqah is due, and for every forty camels a bint labun is due."""
"1848","[AI-translation] Miqdam bin Ma'dikarib, marfu'an: What you feed yourself is a charity for you, and what you feed your child is a charity for you, and what you feed your spouse is a charity for you, and what you feed your servant is a charity for you."
"1849","[AI-translation] From Umm Salama, she said: I used to wear gold ornaments, so I said: ""O Messenger of Allah! Is it a hoard?"" He said: ""Whatever reaches the amount that its zakat is due and is then paid, then it is not a hoard."
"1850","[AI-translation] Darda, marfu'an: No sun has ever risen except that two angels are sent at its sides calling out, making the people of the earth hear except the two weights: ""O people! Come to your Lord, for what is little and sufficient is better than what is much and distracts."" And no sun has ever set except that two angels are sent at its sides calling out, making the people of the earth hear except the two weights: ""O Allah, give a spender a replacement, and give a withholder destruction."
"1851","[AI-translation] From 'Abbad bin Sharhabil, who said: A year of famine struck me, so I entered a garden of the gardens of Al-Madinah, and I rubbed some ears of grain and ate, and carried some in my garment. Its owner came, struck me, and took my garment. I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said to him: ""You did not teach him when he was ignorant, nor did you feed him when he was hungry."" And he ordered him, so he returned my garment to me and gave me a wasq or half a wasq of food."
"1852","[AI-translation] From Sa'sa'a bin Mu'awiya, who said: I met Abu Dharr. I said: ""Narrate to me."" He said: ""Yes. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: 'There is no Muslim servant who spends from every wealth he has a pair in the path of Allah except that the gatekeepers of Paradise meet him, all of them calling him to what is with them.' I said: 'And how is that?' He said: 'If it is camels, then two camels, and if it is cows, then two cows.'"
"1853","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), raised to the Prophet: No day dawns upon the servants except that two angels descend, one of them saying: ""O Allah! Give replacement to the one who spends."" And the other saying: ""O Allah! Give ruin to the one who withholds."
"1854","[AI-translation] From Aisha that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""No wealth has ever benefited us as much as the wealth of Abu Bakr benefited us."
"1855","[AI-translation] From Ibn Burayda from his father, raised to the Prophet: A man does not bring out his charity until he unfastens with it the jaws of seventy devils."
"1856","On the authority of Umm Salamah, that the Prophet ﷺ was in her house with a man from his companions, conversing, when a man came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how much is the zakat due on such-and-such amount of dates?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Such-and-such amount of dates."" The man said: ""So-and-so transgressed against me and took from me such-and-such, adding an extra sa'."" He ﷺ said: ""Then how will it be when someone comes upon you who transgresses against you more severely than this transgression?"" The people engaged in discussion and were astonished by the talk, until a man from them said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if a man is absent from you with his camels, livestock, and crops, and he pays the zakat of his wealth, but the collector transgresses against him, what should he do while he is absent from you?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever pays the zakat of his wealth willingly, seeking the Face of Allah and the Hereafter, nothing of his wealth will be lost, and he establishes prayer and pays zakat, then the collector transgresses against him, so he takes up his weapon and fights until he is killed—he is a martyr."""
"1857","[AI-translation] From Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), raised to the Prophet: Whoever seeks refuge by Allah, give him refuge, and whoever asks you by the Face of Allah, give to him."
"1858","[AI-translation] From Ibn Umar, raised to the Prophet: Whoever seeks refuge by Allah, give him refuge; whoever asks you by Allah, give to him; whoever invites you, answer him; (and whoever seeks protection by Allah, protect him); and whoever does you a favor, recompense him, and if you do not find anything, then supplicate for him until you know that you have recompensed him."
"1859","[AI-translation] From Sulayman ibn Burayda from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: Whoever grants respite to an insolvent debtor, for him is a charity equal to it for every day. He said: Then I heard him say: Whoever grants respite to an insolvent debtor, for him is a charity double that for every day. I said: ""I heard you, O Messenger of Allah, say: Whoever grants respite to an insolvent debtor, for him is a charity equal to it for every day, then I heard you say: Whoever grants respite to an insolvent debtor, for him is a charity double that for every day?"" He said: ""For him is a charity for every day before the debt becomes due, but when the debt becomes due and he grants him respite, then for him is a charity double that for every day."
"1860","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever spends a pair (of something) in the cause of Allah will be called in Paradise: 'O servant of Allah, this is good.' So whoever was among the people of prayer will be called from the gate of prayer; whoever was among the people of jihad will be called from the gate of jihad; whoever was among the people of charity will be called from the gate of charity; and whoever was among the people of fasting will be called from the gate of al-Rayyan."" Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will there be any necessity upon anyone called from those gates? Will anyone be called from all those gates?"" He said: ""Yes, and I hope that you will be among them."""
"1861","[AI-translation] Juhani, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: Whoever is brought a favor by his brother without asking and without the soul desiring it, let him accept it and not reject it, for it is but a provision that Allah has driven to him."
"1862","Hudhayfah said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) during his illness and found him trying to sit up, while Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was with him, nodding off, meaning from drowsiness. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I see that Ali has stayed awake with you this night; shall I not draw near to you?"" He said: ""Ali is more entitled to that."" So Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) drew near to him and supported him, and I heard him say: ""Whoever has as his final deed feeding a poor person, seeking reward from Allah the Mighty and Majestic, will enter Paradise. Whoever has as his final deed fasting a day, seeking reward from Allah, will enter Paradise. Whoever has as his final deed saying 'There is no god but Allah,' seeking reward from Allah, will enter Paradise."""
"1863","[AI-translation] From Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), he wrote to an agent of his on a land of his: ""Do not withhold the surplus of your water, for indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) say: Whoever withholds the surplus of his water or the surplus of his pasture, Allah will withhold His surplus from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"1864","[AI-translation] From Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sa'd came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my mother died and did not make a bequest. Would it benefit her if I gave charity on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes. And upon you is water."
"1865","[AI-translation] Badri (may Allah be pleased with him), raised to the Prophet: The spending of a man upon his family (expecting reward) is a charity."
"1866","[AI-translation] Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Woe to those who hoard,"" except those who say with their wealth, ""like this, and like this, and like this, and like this"" — four [times], to his right, to his left, in front of him, and behind him."
"1867","A man from Banu Asad reported that he said: ""I and my family settled at Baqi' al-Gharqad. My family said to me: 'Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and ask him for something for us to eat.' And they kept mentioning their needs. So I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and found a man with him asking him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was saying: 'I do not find anything to give you.' So the man turned away from him in anger, saying: 'By my life, you indeed give to whom you wish.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'He becomes angry with me because I do not find anything to give him. Whoever among you asks while he has an uqiyah or its equivalent, he has asked importunately.' The Asadi said: 'So I said: A milch camel for us is better than an uqiyah.' Malik said: 'And the uqiyah is forty dirhams.' He said: 'So I returned and did not ask him. Then barley and raisins were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) after that, and he distributed to us from them until Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, made us self-sufficient."""
"1868","Abu Jurayy al-Hujaymi reported: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a people from the desert dwellers, so teach us something that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, may benefit us with."" He said: ""Do not belittle any good deed, even if it is emptying your bucket into the vessel of the one seeking water, or speaking to your brother with a cheerful face. And beware of trailing the lower garment, for it is from arrogance, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not love arrogance. And if a man reviles you with what he knows about you, do not revile him with what you know about him, for his reward is for you and his sin is upon the one who said it."""
"1869","[AI-translation] Narrated Aishah: A dhab was gifted to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) but he did not eat it. Aishah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we not feed it to the poor?"" He said: ""Do not feed them that which you do not eat."
"1870","[AI-translation] Narrated Bahz bin Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather (Mu'awiyah bin Haydah), that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""A man does not come to his mawla asking him for some bounty he has, and he withholds it from him, except that he will be called on the Day of Resurrection a bald snake, licking the bounty he withheld."
"1871","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: A person does not open for himself a door of asking except that Allah opens for him a door of poverty. A man taking his rope, going to the mountain, gathering wood on his back, and eating from it, is better for him than asking people, whether given or denied."
"1872","[AI-translation] Narrated Aishah: The Prophet (peace be upon him) sought the protection of Abu Bakr from Aishah, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not think he would do to her what he did. So Abu Bakr raised his hand and slapped her, and struck her chest. The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) was distressed by that and said: ""O Abu Bakr! I shall never seek your protection from her after this, ever."
"1873","[AI-translation] Narrated Ubaydullah bin Abbas, he said: Abu Dharr said to me: ""O son of my brother, I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) holding his hand, and he said: 'O Abu Dharr, I would not love to have any gold or silver to spend in the path of Allah, and die on the day I die, leaving from it a qirat.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a qintar?' He said: 'O Abu Dharr, I go to the lesser and you go to the greater?! I want the Hereafter and you want the world?! A qirat!' He repeated it to me three times."
"1874","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Qatadah, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""O people! Buy yourselves from Allah with the wealth of Allah. If one of you is stingy to give his wealth to the people, let him start with himself and give charity to himself, let him eat and clothe himself from what Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, has provided him."
"1875","On the authority of Fatimah bint Qays, she said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ with a necklace containing seventy mithqals of gold, and I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, take from it the obligatory due (zakah) that Allah has ordained in it."" She said: So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took one mithqal and three-quarters of a mithqal and set it aside. She said: Then I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, take from it that which Allah has ordained in it."" She said: So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed it among these six categories and others, then he said: ""O Fatimah! Verily, the Truth (Allah, Mighty and Majestic) has not left anything for you."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am content for myself with what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger ﷺ are content with."""
"1876","Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came to us and said: ""O company of Emigrants, there are five things with which you will be tested, and I seek refuge in Allah that you should live to see them: Immorality never appears among a people to the extent that they publicize it, except that plague and diseases that were not known among their predecessors who passed away will spread among them. They never cheat in weights and measures except that they will be stricken with years of drought, severe hardship, and the oppression of their rulers. They never withhold the zakat of their wealth except that rain will be withheld from the sky, and were it not for the animals, they would not be given rain. They never break the covenant of Allah and His Messenger except that Allah will empower an enemy from outside them to take some of what is in their hands. And their leaders do not rule by the Book of Allah and choose from what Allah has revealed except that Allah will make their might turn against each other."""
"1877","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) that he intended to go on a military expedition and said: ""O assembly of Muhajirun and Ansar, among your brothers are people who have neither wealth nor kin. Let one of you attach to himself two or three men."" Jabir said: ""So none of us had a mount to carry him except a turn like the turn of one of them,"" meaning one of them. He said: ""I attached to myself two or three."" He said: ""I had nothing but a turn like the turn of one of them on my camel."
"1878","On the authority of Busr ibn Jahhash al-Qurashi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recited this verse: ""But what is the matter with those who disbelieve, hastening toward you, [in crowds] on the right and on the left, in groups? Does every man of them hope to enter the Garden of Bliss? No! Indeed, We created them from what they know."" (Qur'an 70:36-39)Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) spat on his palm and said: ""Allah says: 'O son of Adam, how can you escape Me when I created you from something like this? Until when I fashioned you and proportioned you, you walked between two garments, and the earth complained of you—meaning: complaint—so you gathered and withheld until when it reached the collarbones, you said: ""I will give charity,"" but when is the time for charity?'"""
"1879","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) who said: The treasure of one of you will become on the Day of Resurrection a bald snake, fleeing from its owner, so he will seek it and it will say: ""I am your treasure."" He said: By Allah, it will not cease seeking him until he stretches out his hand, and it will put it into its mouth."
